From 30af84915c779bcdf15e3d404930b3af09762ad0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rehanraza786 <rehanraza786@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 03:32:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] Tool-selection eval: measure what the description trim
 was spending
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The previous commit cut descriptions 46% and admitted the obvious gap: the
routing eval pins skills to utterances, and nothing pinned tools. A budget
ratchet with no opposing force only ever trades tokens for accuracy, and
never says so.

22 request -> tool cases, weighted toward the clusters that actually collide:
the four symbol lookups, the three composites, the three seam maps,
explain vs explain_path, graph_gaps vs assert_edge. Scored by stemmed term
overlap against every tool's description, and a TIE COUNTS AS A FAILURE,
because two tools the words cannot separate is the thing being measured.
Plus a per-cluster check that each member owns a term no sibling has.

This is a proxy, not a model eval, and the commit should say so: it proves
the discriminating vocabulary is present, not that a model uses it well. A
model may do better than term overlap. It cannot do better with words that
are gone.

It found real damage immediately. After the trim, find_references and
find_callers were separated by nothing an agent could use ("is this used,
I need to be certain before deleting" scored them equal), db_map had lost
the word changelog that its own drift warning is about, http_map no longer
said endpoints nobody calls, decisions could not match the word decide, and
graph_gaps and assert_edge each described the other's job. Ten descriptions
got their discriminating vocabulary back, at a cost of 356 bytes.

It also found a flaw in itself: the first stoplist filtered use/used/call/
find as noise words. Those are the most discriminating verbs in this domain
— the filter was deleting exactly the signal it existed to measure.

Net 6,971 -> 4,148 bytes, and now bounded on both sides: the ratchet is the
ceiling, the eval is the floor, and descriptions can get shorter only until
they stop working.
